  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nIR shows cooling cloud tops across the CWA as the tropical \nmoisture has begun to interact with the open wave pushing up \nfrom the southwest nearing the CWA. WV imagery shows a \ndisjointed PFJ dipping around the trough to the west but mainly \nto the north into southern Canada with a 120-130kt jet max \nhelping to aid the aforementioned system that will affect the \nCWA throughout Friday and lingering into Saturday. Radar shows a\nfew scattered light showers pushing across Natrona County that \nwill diminish before sunrise Friday morning. The main push of \nprecipitation will come from eastern Idaho and northern Utah \nlater through the overnight hours that will eventually spread \neast to the Divide later in the morning. Some rumbles of thunder\nare possible with instability values in the 100-200 J/kg range \ninto the afternoon. PVA anomalies look to be more constrained \nover Idaho and into southern Montana, limiting any significant \naccumulations but limited mountain snow is likely for the higher\nelevations of the western mountains. More prominent for the \nTetons and into Yellowstone, 3 to 5 inches are possible but with\nlower confidence at this time (30-40%). Freezing levels will \nremain above 10kft ahead of the cold front later in the \nafternoon with limited precipitation at that time of FROPA. \n\nWinds will be of concern today remaining elevated in many areas\ninto Friday morning already, with a possible weak mountain wave\noff the southern Winds into the eastern parts of the Wind River\nBasin. Gusts up to 30 to 35 mph are possible with this but not \nof high concern at this time. Winds will increase through the \nlate Friday morning hours as the gradient continues to tighten \nahead of FROPA. Southwest gusts up to 40 to 55 mph are likely \nfor much of the area, strongest east of the Divide. HiRes models\nindicate the front to push into Yellowstone by around 4PM and \npushing southeast through the evening hours, reaching east of \nthe Bighorns by 6-7PM where the highest of fire weather concerns\nwill be. Moisture will be limited east of the Divide, but areas\naround the Cody Foothills off the Absarokas will have the best \nchance for precipitation with frontal passage, outside of the \nupslope flow of the Bighorns to Owl Creeks/Bridgers. Minimal \nhigh elevation snow and limited in the valley rain of \nsignificance but around a 20 percent coverage on this occurrence\nheading towards sunset Friday evening. By sunrise Saturday \nmorning, any shower activity will have diminished in the wake of\nthe cold front as this system has continued to underperform \nwith the models with its weaker trajectory over time this past \nweek. Being indicative of the winter to come in the stronger El \nNino pattern, it could be a recurring theme going forward and \nespecially east of the Divide. \n\nBeyond that, temperatures Friday afternoon will push 10 to 20 \ndegrees above average due to the suppressional heating ahead of\nthe aforementioned cold front. Behind it, expect more \nseasonable to just below average temperatures over the weekend \ninto early next week to feel more Fall-like heading into the \nlast week of October in Wyoming. Behind this quick pushing \nsystem into the northern Great Plains, expect induced ridging \nfor the later half of the weekend ahead of the next bowling ball\nof a low coming from the Gulf of Alaska. By Monday morning, \nexpect it to reach the Pacific Northwest remaining a closed low \nthat will begin to affect the CWA and in particular for the west\nby Tuesday. This will be more inclined for western mountain \nsnow and reinforcing the cooler air to allow for even some \nvalley snow west of the Divide. Time will tell, but models show \na disjointed system with much disagreement still to come. Stay \ntuned for updated forecasts beyond this system for Friday in the\ncoming days.",
